"""LiteLLM custom callback: a reject-fast, per-caller session lock for the
RTX 4080's single llama.cpp slot (guest llm-4080).

Deployed by roles/llm_router next to config.yaml, and resolved by LiteLLM
from litellm_settings.callbacks: "fast_subagent_lock.proxy_handler_instance"
(config.yaml.j2) — LiteLLM imports a callback module relative to the config
file's own directory, so this file must live beside config.yaml. NOT smoke-
tested against a live proxy in the PR that added this; see the PR body.

Rationale for the lock, the model set it covers, and the TTL:
roles/llm_router/defaults/main/57-subagent-lock.yml.

TWO DIFFERENT BEHAVIORS ON CONTENTION, by how the caller named the request:

  * A direct call (GATED_MODELS — hermes-local-4080, either vllm-tier id, or
    the `fast-gpu` DB role, which is unioned in for exactly this hard-429
    treatment; see 57-subagent-lock.yml for why it must not redirect) gets
    the hard 429 above. Unchanged from the first version of this file.

  * A `fast`/`subagent` role call (ROLE_NAMES) REDIRECTS onto
    ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET instead of rejecting. This is deliberately NOT "raise
    the same 429 and let LiteLLM's Router fall back" — `async_pre_call_hook`
    runs in the PROXY layer (`proxy_logging_obj.pre_call_hook`, called before
    `llm_router.acompletion()`), before the Router's own fallback/cooldown
    engine is ever entered, so an exception raised here propagates straight to
    the client and CANNOT trigger a Router-level fallback, regardless of
    exception type (a `litellm.RateLimitError` would not behave differently
    here than the `HTTPException` above — neither ever reaches the Router).
    This is the author's best understanding of the pinned litellm[proxy]
    architecture, NOT verified against a live process in this PR — confirm
    before trusting it under load. Rewriting `data["model"]` here, before
    dispatch, is the mechanism `async_pre_call_hook` actually supports for
    changing what a request hits.

    ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET is TEMPLATED AT CONVERGE TIME (the chain's own
    second rung — see 55-roles.yml's `_llm_router_fast_subagent_chain`), not
    read live from the role's own DB-stored fallback order. An operator who
    reorders `fast`/`subagent` in the Admin UI changes the ORDINARY (non-
    contention) fallback path immediately — that is plain LiteLLM role
    routing, unaffected by this file — but does NOT change what THIS
    callback redirects to on contention specifically, until the next
    converge re-renders this file. Flagged as a real limitation, not solved
    here: a live lookup would need either a second network hop to this same
    proxy's own management API on every contended request, or an in-process
    read of LiteLLM's own Router object, and neither was verifiable without a
    running proxy.

ACQUISITION HAPPENS BEFORE THE ROUTER PICKS A RUNG, which is a known,
partial limitation, not a solved one. `async_pre_call_hook` fires once per
REQUEST — before litellm has attempted any deployment — so it cannot tell
whether THIS specific attempt will actually reach hermes-local-4080's
deployment (today it always does: hermes-local-4080 is `fast`'s and
`subagent`'s ONLY deployment, so the first attempt always targets it, but
that is a fact about the CURRENT converge, not something this file can
verify at runtime — see the redirect-target limitation above for the same
root cause). What this file DOES do about it: hold the lock for exactly as
long as the underlying work is in flight, not the full TTL. Every
`fast`/`subagent` acquisition auto-releases on that SAME request's own
completion (success or failure), via async_post_call_success_hook /
async_post_call_failure_hook below — so a caller that finishes in 5s frees
the slot in 5s, not 300s, EVEN IF the Router's own ordinary fallback chain
carried the request past hermes-local-4080 to a later rung (Mac Studio /
cloud / long) without this file's involvement — release does not depend on
which rung actually served the request. This does not change WHEN the lock
is taken, only how long it is held, which is what actually matters for
"does an idle 4080 lock out fast-gpu/direct callers" — that window is now
bounded by one request's wall-clock time, not the TTL. A genuine
acquisition-time fix (gate on the Router's actually-resolved deployment, not
the caller-typed name) would need a hook this file could not identify with
confidence.

WHY RELEASE READS data["metadata"], NOT data["model"]. The first version of
this fix keyed release eligibility off `data.get("model")`, matching it
against ROLE_NAMES/GATED_MODELS the same way the pre-call hook does. That
breaks the moment the Router's own fallback machinery rewrites `data["model"]`
to reflect whichever deployment actually served the request (LiteLLM's
documented pattern for request logging) — a `fast` call that fell through to
a cloud rung would then read back as some unrelated model id at post-call
time, `is_role_call` would evaluate false, and `_maybe_release` would skip
entirely: the exact "holds the lock for the full 300s while the 4080 sits
idle" failure this fix exists to prevent, and the one that makes `fast-gpu`'s
429-means-busy signal unreliable for every caller told to trust it. Fixed by
stamping the caller shape ("role" vs "direct") into `data["metadata"]` in
`async_pre_call_hook`, before anything downstream has a chance to touch
`data["model"]`, and reading that stamp back in `_maybe_release` instead.
`metadata` is litellm's own documented pass-through field for exactly this
kind of request-scoped custom data, and unlike `model` nothing else in the
request lifecycle has a reason to overwrite it.

Direct callers (GATED_MODELS) do NOT auto-release on completion — that
would break the ORIGINAL feature this lock exists for (a caller like Hermes
holding the lock across several repeat calls to keep the KV cache warm,
57-subagent-lock.yml). Auto-release is scoped to ROLE_NAMES only, because
`fast`/`subagent` calls are single-shot ad hoc delegation by design (no
multi-call session), so releasing after the one call that used the lock is
correct there and would be wrong for a direct caller.

Both the refresh-if-same-holder and release-if-same-holder operations use a
Lua script (single Redis round trip: GET-compare-then-EXPIRE/DEL
atomically) instead of separate GET-then-EXPIRE / GET-then-DELETE calls —
the two-call form has a TOCTOU window where a DIFFERENT, newer holder's
lock could be refreshed or deleted by a caller who no longer holds it,
between the GET and the second call.
"""

import os

from fastapi import HTTPException
from litellm.integrations.custom_logger import CustomLogger

try:
    import redis.asyncio as redis_asyncio
except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - always present inside the litellm[proxy] venv
    redis_asyncio = None

LOCK_KEY = {{ llm_router_subagent_lock_key | to_json }}
LOCK_TTL_SECONDS = {{ llm_router_subagent_lock_ttl_seconds }}
RELEASE_HEADER = {{ llm_router_subagent_lock_release_header | to_json }}
# The client_model_id set that shares the 4080's single llama.cpp slot and KV
# cache — derived from the registry by tier, plus the `fast-gpu` DB role
# name, see 57-subagent-lock.yml.
GATED_MODELS = frozenset({{ llm_router_subagent_lock_model_ids | to_json }})
# DB-seeded role names whose calls redirect around contention instead of
# hard-rejecting — see 55-roles.yml and the module docstring above.
ROLE_NAMES = frozenset({{ llm_router_subagent_lock_role_names | to_json }})
ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET = {{ llm_router_primary_model | to_json }}
# metadata key _maybe_release reads to learn the caller shape (role vs
# direct) that async_pre_call_hook determined — see the module docstring for
# why this cannot be re-derived from data["model"] at post-call time.
_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY = "subagent_lock_caller_shape"

# Same connection this proxy already uses for spend tracking (router_settings
# in config.yaml.j2): host/password via the systemd EnvironmentFile, port as
# a non-secret literal baked in at render time — reusing that pattern rather
# than declaring a second Redis config.
_REDIS_HOST = os.environ.get("LLM_ROUTER_REDIS_HOST", "")
_REDIS_PORT = {{ llm_router_redis_port }}
_REDIS_PASSWORD = os.environ.get("LLM_ROUTER_REDIS_PASSWORD") or None


# Atomic compare-and-refresh: extends the TTL only if the caller asking is
# still the recorded holder. Returns 1 on refresh, 0 if the key is absent or
# held by someone else (never raises for "not the holder" — that is an
# ordinary, expected outcome, not an error).
_REFRESH_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT = """
if redis.call("GET", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  return redis.call("EXPIRE", KEYS[1], ARGV[2])
else
  return 0
end
"""

# Atomic compare-and-delete: same shape, for release instead of refresh.
_RELEASE_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT = """
if redis.call("GET", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  return redis.call("DEL", KEYS[1])
else
  return 0
end
"""


def _caller_id(user_api_key_dict) -> str:
    """The identity a lock decision is made against — a virtual key's alias
    (roles/llm_router/defaults/main/56-virtual-keys.yml), falling back to the
    key's own token for an unaliased/master-key caller so the lock still
    degrades to a real (if less readable) identity rather than colliding
    every unattributed caller onto the literal string "unknown"."""
    return (
        getattr(user_api_key_dict, "key_alias", None)
        or getattr(user_api_key_dict, "token", None)
        or "unknown"
    )


def _release_requested(data: dict) -> bool:
    headers = (data.get("proxy_server_request") or {}).get("headers") or {}
    return str(headers.get(RELEASE_HEADER, "")).strip().lower() in ("1", "true", "yes")


class FastSubagentLock(CustomLogger):
    """One caller at a time on the 4080. A direct caller (GATED_MODELS) that
    loses the race is rejected with 429 immediately — no wait-queue, no
    preemption; it holds the lock across repeat calls (cache-affinity across
    a session), released only by the 300s TTL or an explicit
    X-Subagent-Release. A fast/subagent role caller (ROLE_NAMES) that loses
    the race is silently redirected instead, and — unlike a direct caller —
    auto-releases the lock on its OWN call's completion, success or failure:
    see the module docstring for why the two callers need different release
    semantics."""

    def __init__(self, client=None):
        # Injectable for tests (tests/llm_router/test_fast_subagent_lock.py);
        # production always lazy-connects via _get_client below.
        self._client = client

    def _get_client(self):
        if self._client is not None:
            return self._client
        if redis_asyncio is None:
            raise RuntimeError(
                "redis.asyncio is unavailable — expected inside the litellm[proxy] venv"
            )
        self._client = redis_asyncio.Redis(
            host=_REDIS_HOST,
            port=_REDIS_PORT,
            password=_REDIS_PASSWORD,
            decode_responses=True,
        )
        return self._client

    async def _refresh_if_holder(self, client, caller_id: str) -> bool:
        return bool(await client.eval(_REFRESH_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT, 1, LOCK_KEY, caller_id, LOCK_TTL_SECONDS))

    async def _release_if_holder(self, client, caller_id: str) -> bool:
        return bool(await client.eval(_RELEASE_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT, 1, LOCK_KEY, caller_id))

    async def async_pre_call_hook(self, user_api_key_dict, cache, data: dict, call_type: str):
        model = data.get("model")
        is_role_call = model in ROLE_NAMES
        if model not in GATED_MODELS and not is_role_call:
            return data
        # Stamped BEFORE anything downstream (the Router's own fallback
        # machinery included) gets a chance to rewrite data["model"] — see
        # the module docstring. _maybe_release reads this, never data["model"].
        data.setdefault("metadata", {})[_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY] = "role" if is_role_call else "direct"
        caller_id = _caller_id(user_api_key_dict)
        client = self._get_client()
        # NX+EX in one round trip: acquires only if absent, sets the TTL as
        # a side effect of the very acquisition that succeeds.
        acquired = await client.set(LOCK_KEY, caller_id, nx=True, ex=LOCK_TTL_SECONDS)
        if acquired:
            return data
        if await self._refresh_if_holder(client, caller_id):
            # Same caller, still working — refreshed so continued use keeps
            # the lock alive instead of expiring mid-session.
            return data
        if is_role_call:
            # See the module docstring: redirect, not raise — an exception
            # here cannot reach the Router's own fallback engine.
            data["model"] = ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET
            return data
        holder = await client.get(LOCK_KEY)
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=429,
            detail={
                "error": "llm-4080 is locked by another caller",
                "held_by": holder,
                "retry_after_seconds": LOCK_TTL_SECONDS,
            },
            headers={"Retry-After": str(LOCK_TTL_SECONDS)},
        )

    async def _maybe_release(self, data: dict, user_api_key_dict) -> None:
        """Shared by the success and failure post-call hooks — see the
        module docstring for why role calls auto-release unconditionally
        while direct calls release only on the explicit header, and for why
        this reads data["metadata"] rather than data["model"]."""
        shape = (data.get("metadata") or {}).get(_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY)
        if shape is None:
            return
        is_role_call = shape == "role"
        if not is_role_call and not _release_requested(data):
            return
        caller_id = _caller_id(user_api_key_dict)
        client = self._get_client()
        await self._release_if_holder(client, caller_id)

    async def async_post_call_success_hook(self, data: dict, user_api_key_dict, response):
        await self._maybe_release(data, user_api_key_dict)
        return response

    # Signature confirmed against LiteLLM's own docs (docs.litellm.ai/docs/
    # proxy/call_hooks, fetched for this PR) — NOT verified against a live
    # process on the pinned 1.98.0 release specifically. Still wrapped
    # defensively: a documented-but-wrong-on-this-pin signature must degrade
    # to "the lock is not released early" (falls back to the 300s TTL, the
    # pre-existing safety net), never to an exception that could interfere
    # with the actual failure response reaching the caller. Returning None
    # (rather than an HTTPException) leaves the original error untouched, as
    # documented.
    async def async_post_call_failure_hook(
        self, request_data: dict, original_exception: Exception, user_api_key_dict, traceback_str=None
    ):
        try:
            await self._maybe_release(request_data, user_api_key_dict)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - see docstring: must never break the failure path
            pass
        return None


proxy_handler_instance = FastSubagentLock()
